.TH FFT 3 "2026-09-21" "BigNums" "Library Functions Manual"
.nr HY 0
.ds AD l
.SH NAME
fft, fft_arbitrary \- transform an array of complex numbers in place
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqcomplexFFT.h\(dq
.sp
.BI "int fft(complexNum *" "x" ", int " "n" ", int " "inverse" ");"
.sp
.BI "int fft_arbitrary(complexNum *" "x" ", int " "n" ", int " "inverse" ");"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The array x must contain n initialized complexNum elements, each with double
fields re and im.  The transform overwrites x.
A zero inverse argument selects the forward transform with a negative
exponential sign; any nonzero value selects the inverse with a positive sign
and division by n.
.PP
.B fft()
requires a positive power-of-two length and performs an iterative radix-2 FFT.
.B fft_arbitrary()
uses that fast path for powers of two and Bluestein convolution otherwise.
Bluestein temporary buffers are allocated internally and freed before return.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.B fft()
returns 0 for valid inputs.
.B fft_arbitrary()
returns 0 on success or \-1 for a nonpositive length or allocation failure.
.SH BUGS
.B fft()
does not validate its length.  Both routines assume valid pointers and array
bounds.  Very large lengths can overflow signed size calculations; such
lengths are unsupported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bigIntMulFFT (3),
.BR bignums (3),
.BR bignums (7)
